<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Icon drawn on the Android 12+ system splash (windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon).

     Deliberately our own resource instead of letting the platform fall back to
     @mipmap/ic_launcher. Without this attribute the splash asks the icon
     provider for the launcher icon, and the shell hands back the *themed*
     icon: One UI icon themes compose their own plate behind every third-party
     icon. On the Coordinator's Galaxy A72 the active theme
     (com.gabriel.SeashellPink.appicon) supplies ic_icon_bg.png — a white
     rounded square at 50% alpha — which over our beige sheet reads as a pale
     plate around the cockade, i.e. a visible seam before the Flutter splash
     takes over. A drawable loaded straight from the window theme skips that
     composition path.

     The artwork is not duplicated: this reuses the adaptive icon's foreground
     layer, whose cockade occupies the central 66dp of the 108dp canvas.

     The inset is the scale, not padding. Undrawn, the platform renders that
     cockade at 189dp on a 411dp-wide screen, while the Flutter splash draws
     its own cockade at 2·min(0.159·W, 72) = 131dp — so the mark would visibly
     shrink at the handoff. 15.3% per side scales 189dp down onto 131dp,
     measured on the A72. A fraction rather than a dimension so the ratio
     survives a differently sized icon window; the two rules still diverge on
     very wide screens, where the Flutter side caps at 144dp. -->
<inset xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground"
    android:inset="15.3%" />
